0387162235?s=20\",\"children\":\"swarms of AI coding agents\"}],\", sometimes mentioning concepts like \",[\"$\",\"$L64\",null,{\"href\":\"https://x.com/bcherny/status/2025007393290272904?s=20\",\"children\":\"Git worktrees\"}],\". If you’ve already been running Claude or another agent in your terminal, you might be wondering: \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"what exactly are worktrees, and why do they matter?\"}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Think about what usually happens when you run an AI agent to write code. You start it, then wait watching the terminal scroll, maybe checking your phone, or doing something unrelated. In other words, you’re idle while the agent works. What if instead of waiting, you could assign multiple tasks to multiple agents and have them all working in parallel?\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The idea is appealing: \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"If one agent can build a feature, why not run several agents to build multiple features at once?\"}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"At first, it seems straightforward. Open a few terminals, start your agents, and assign tasks in parallel. But in practice, things break down quickly:\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"ol\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"File collisions:\"}],\" Agents overwrite each other’s changes.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Git conflicts:\"}],\" Shared \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\".git\"}],\" state leads to locked indexes or tangled commits.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Database conflicts:\"}],\" Tests or migrations run in parallel against the same database, causing failures.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The underlying issue is isolation. Without separate environments, agents interfere with each other’s work, making parallel development unreliable. There are brute-force fixes: cloning your repository multiple times or running separate Docker containers for each database. But those approaches are slow, resource-heavy, and add unnecessary friction.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"A more efficient solution is to give each agent its own lightweight workspace and database branch. That’s where Git worktrees and Neon come in. Worktrees let you create multiple working directories from a single repository without duplication, while Neon provides instant database branches that keep migrations and tests separate. Together, they make it practical to run agents in parallel without collisions.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In this guide, we’ll walk through how to set up a workflow that allows multiple agents to run side by side, each with its own isolated environment. We’ll cover:\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Git worktrees\"}],\" for clean file system separation\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Neon\"}],\" for instant, independent database branches\"]}],\"\\n\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Finally, we’ll tie it all together with a custom Git hook so that spinning up a new agent with its own database takes only a single command.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"the-solution-part-1-git-worktrees\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"The Solution Part 1: Git Worktrees\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#the-solution-part-1-git-worktrees\",\"a